# Every file is checked out exactly as it was committed, on every platform.
#
# Much of this repository is bytes that something pins: vectors the code regenerates and compares,
# signed documents such as the model profiles and the plugin catalogue, payloads a lock names by
# digest such as the bundled plugins and the contact skill, generated schemas, and the shell
# packages, startup files and scripts a test runs. A checkout that rewrote line endings, as Git does
# on Windows with core.autocrlf set, would hand each of them bytes other than those that were
# signed, hashed or run. Every text file here is committed with LF endings and none needs another,
# so one rule for every file is the rule that still holds when the next pinned file is added.
* -text

# The one exception is a batch file, which cmd.exe runs. cmd.exe finds a label by reading the file
# itself in blocks and relies on CRLF line ends to do it, so a batch file checked out with LF endings
# can fail a `goto` or a `call :label` depending on where its lines happen to fall. A batch file is
# committed with LF endings like everything else and checked out with CRLF endings on every
# platform, so the bytes cmd.exe reads are the ones it expects.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
